# OmniStudio deploys = mandatory cache-bust dance

**OmniStudio runs from compiled artifacts cached in `OmniProcessCompilation` rows, not from the metadata you just deployed.** A vanilla `sf project deploy start` doesn't reliably invalidate that cache. **Either delete the compilation row OR run the full 5-step deactivate/reactivate dance** before assuming your deploy took effect. Apply this for **every deploy of an active OmniProcess version** — not "when symptoms appear" (by the time probes don't fire you've already burned an hour debugging working code).

> Retrieve before editing ([`retrieve-before-edit.mdc`](./retrieve-before-edit.mdc)) — OmniStudio metadata drifts especially fast because admins edit IPs/DRs directly in the org UI. Companion skill: [`.claude/skills/omnistudio-deploy-cache-bust/SKILL.md`](../../.claude/skills/omnistudio-deploy-cache-bust/SKILL.md) — keep both in sync.

## The cache model (the WHY)

- Every OmniScript / IP / FlexCard is one **`OmniProcess`** row (`UniqueName = <Type>_<SubType>_<Lang>_<Version>`, or `<Type>_<SubType>_Procedure_<Version>` for IPs); its steps are **`OmniProcessElement`** rows.
- On activation (`IsActive: false → true`) the package compiles the element tree into one **`OmniProcessCompilation`** row (`OmniProcessId = <OmniProcess.Id>`). The runtime reads THAT, not the metadata. Until it's invalidated, your edits are invisible.
- IPs also have an **`OmniIntegrationProcConfig`** row (`DeveloperName = <UniqueName>`) that callers reference — this is why deleting an actively-called IP throws `DEPENDENCY_EXISTS`.
- **DataRaptors live in `OmniDataTransform`, are interpreted, and have NO compilation cache — skip this entire rule for `*.rpt-meta.xml`.** (Caveat: a DR change can still look invisible if its *caller* is stale-cached — bust the caller.)

> 🚨 **Caller-side cache — the trap that catches everyone.** When OmniProcess A calls B (a "Run Integration Procedure" step), A's compilation embeds a snapshot of B's compiled artifact. Busting B alone is NOT enough — A keeps serving the old B. **Bust the entire caller chain from the entry point down to your edited component** (§ Caller chain below).

## When to apply / skip

**Apply** when: deploying a manifest containing `OmniScript`/`OmniIntegrationProcedure`/any `Omni*`; you edited a file under `omniScripts/`, `omniIntegrationProcedures/`, or a FlexCard; adding probes; changing a conditional formula, input mapping, response JSON path, or remote-action class on an active version; renaming/reordering elements; swapping a DR/Apex/IP bundle; or migrating where the component already exists in the target.

**Skip** when: deploying a brand-new version that never existed (first creation always recompiles); a version whose file is `<isActive>false</isActive>`; pure DataRaptor changes; pure CustomField/ValidationRule/Layout/Apex changes touching no Omni XML; fresh scratch/sandbox bootstrap.

**Symptoms the cache is stale** (run the dance, don't debug your code): a just-added probe/Set-Values/Remote-Action isn't in the IP debug response; a conditional fires/skips against your edited `executionConditionalFormula`; a DR call uses the old `bundle`; a renamed step still shows the old name; two identical-input runs differ; description tweak deploys as `Unchanged`.

## Path A — minimal (3 steps, low-risk edits)

Use for a small diagnostic edit when nothing else depends on the in-flight cache, or after a recent successful Path B.

```bash
# 1. Delete the cached compilation
sf data query -o {{ORG_ALIAS}} --query "SELECT Id FROM OmniProcessCompilation WHERE OmniProcessId = '<Vcurrent.Id>'"
sf data delete record --sobject OmniProcessCompilation --record-id '<compilation.Id>' -o {{ORG_ALIAS}}
# 2. Tweak the top-level <description> (flip one '.' / ',' to force a byte change) in the IDE
# 3. Redeploy
sf project deploy start --manifest manifest/<feature>.xml -o {{ORG_ALIAS}} --ignore-conflicts --wait 5
```

Run the self-check. If it fails, escalate to Path B.

## Path B — full 5-step dance (non-trivial changes, or Path A failed)

`Vcurrent` = the version you want active; `Vold` = the highest older version of the same `UniqueName` (so you can re-register cleanly).

> **Two package constraints (both `FIELD_INTEGRITY_EXCEPTION`) dictate the step order:** (1) you can't update/delete an ACTIVE OmniProcess — deactivate first; (2) only ONE version per Type/SubType/Language can be active at a time.

```bash
# Step 1 — survey, capture both Ids
sf data query -o {{ORG_ALIAS}} --query "SELECT Id, UniqueName, IsActive, VersionNumber, LastModifiedDate FROM OmniProcess WHERE UniqueName LIKE '<Type>_<SubType>_%' ORDER BY VersionNumber DESC"

# Step 2 — deactivate Vcurrent, then activate Vold (sequential updates in ONE apex run; --file avoids shell heredoc quoting bugs)
cat > /tmp/swap.apex <<'EOF'
update new OmniProcess(Id = '<Vcurrent.Id>', IsActive = false);
update new OmniProcess(Id = '<Vold.Id>',     IsActive = true);
EOF
sf apex run -o {{ORG_ALIAS}} --file /tmp/swap.apex

# Step 3a — delete Vcurrent's compilation (the actual cached artifact — MANDATORY)
sf data delete record --sobject OmniProcessCompilation --record-id '<compilation.Id>' -o {{ORG_ALIAS}}
# Step 3b/3c — try to delete the OmniIntegrationProcConfig row, then the OmniProcess row.
#   Both often fail with DEPENDENCY_EXISTS when an upstream caller references the IP — that's fine, skip.
#   🚨 NEVER rm / git rm the local .oip/.os/.rpt file — you redeploy from local in Step 5.

# Step 4 — deactivate Vold (now no version is active → clean re-registration in Step 5)
cat > /tmp/deact.apex <<'EOF'
update new OmniProcess(Id = '<Vold.Id>', IsActive = false);
EOF
sf apex run -o {{ORG_ALIAS}} --file /tmp/deact.apex

# Step 5 — tweak the top-level <description> by one char, confirm <isActive>true</isActive>, redeploy
sf project deploy start --manifest manifest/<feature>.xml -o {{ORG_ALIAS}} --ignore-conflicts --wait 5
# Deploy summary must read State: Changed (or Created). If Unchanged, the description tweak didn't hit disk — redo.
```

Don't batch the Step 2 updates into one `update new List<>{...}` — the per-record active-check can fail on Vold in arbitrary order. Don't modify upstream callers just to enable 3b/3c — Step 3a is the invalidation that matters.

## Caller chain — the #1 reason "the dance worked but probes still don't fire"

Editing a callee does NOT invalidate caller compilations — there's no automatic cascade. Bust every OmniProcess from the entry point down to your edit (Path A compilation-delete on each):

```bash
# Find IPs whose elements reference your edited IP
sf data query -o {{ORG_ALIAS}} --query "SELECT OmniProcess.UniqueName, Name FROM OmniProcessElement WHERE PropertySetConfig LIKE '%<callee_short_name>%' AND Type = 'Integration Procedure Action' AND OmniProcess.IsActive = true"
# Then delete all their compilations in one apex run:
cat > /tmp/nuke-chain.apex <<'EOF'
delete [SELECT Id FROM OmniProcessCompilation WHERE OmniProcessId IN ('<entry>','<mid>','<edited>')];
EOF
sf apex run -o {{ORG_ALIAS}} --file /tmp/nuke-chain.apex
```

Diagnostic: if the parent IP's `OmniProcessCompilation.LastModifiedDate` predates your edit's activation, the parent is serving stale — that's the culprit. When in doubt, nuke all active compilations older than ~2h (`delete [SELECT Id FROM OmniProcessCompilation WHERE OmniProcess.IsActive = true AND LastModifiedDate < :Datetime.now().addHours(-2)]`) — use sparingly (every active IP pays a recompile). For a multi-component feature, bust **leaves first** (callee → callers → top OmniScript) or do one bulk compilation-delete + one bulk redeploy.

## Edge cases

- **Deploy still `Unchanged` at Step 5:** an editor "format on save" reverted your description tweak — `grep "<description>" <file>` to confirm, disable format-on-save for `*.oip/.os-meta.xml`.
- **No Vold exists locally:** snapshot the file, copy it to an unused version number with `<isActive>false</isActive>`, deploy that as a synthetic Vold, restore the original, run Path B.
- **OmniScripts add a browser cache layer:** after the server-side bust, hard-refresh (Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+R) the host Lightning page, or sign out/in.

## Self-check (before declaring success)

- [ ] `OmniProcess`: Vcurrent `IsActive=true`, `LastModifiedDate` within the last minute.
- [ ] `OmniProcessElement`: your changed elements have NEW `Id`s.
- [ ] `OmniProcessCompilation`: **0 rows** for Vcurrent (recompiles fresh on next invocation).
- [ ] If probes write to a sink, trigger the flow and confirm the probe label appears.
- [ ] `git diff` shows only your intended change + the description byte-flip.

## Common errors (literal strings)

| Error / symptom | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `FIELD_INTEGRITY_EXCEPTION: You can't update or delete an active Omniscript record...` | Touched Vcurrent while active | Deactivate it first (Step 2) |
| `FIELD_INTEGRITY_EXCEPTION: Another active Omniscript with the same Type, Subtype, and Language exists.` | Activated Vold while Vcurrent still active | Deactivate Vcurrent + activate Vold in ONE apex run |
| `DEPENDENCY_EXISTS: ... Omni Integration Procedure Configuration` | Upstream caller references this IP | Skip 3b/3c; rely on the Step 3a compilation delete |
| `Status: Succeeded` + `State: Unchanged` | File matches org byte-for-byte | Description tweak didn't persist — re-edit |
| `No source-backed components present...` | Manifest member used slash syntax | Use `<Type>_<SubType>_Procedure_<N>` underscores |
| `sObject type 'OmniProcess' is not supported.` | Used `--use-tooling-api` | Drop it (standard data API) |
| Probe never appears in its sink | Cache not busted, or wrong block | Add an unconditional top-level probe; if even that fails, it's cache |

## Why it works

The runtime caches the deserialized `OmniProcessCompilation` payload keyed on Type/SubType. Invalidation reliability: **deleting the compilation row (Step 3a) = highest**; `IsActive false→true` flip on redeploy (Step 5) = high; a plain redeploy with neither = very low. The 5-step dance does both, so it's bulletproof. The description byte-flip is the safety net that forces MDAPI to emit `Changed` (and thus fire the package's post-deploy hooks) instead of short-circuiting on `Unchanged`.
